   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Indeed, you are freely estimating the same parameters in both models:</div><div><br></div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">fit6 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> std</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lv </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>fit7 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>all</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sort</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">names</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">coef</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">==</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sort</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">names</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">coef</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))))</span></div></code></div><div><br>I still don&#39;t understand why changing the order of estimated parameters would change convergence, but I think that might just be a coincidence.  You said model6 can be estimated fine, but that came with warnings about Heywood cases, and when you identify the model by fixing factor variances instead of factor loadings, both models converge but cannot estimate <i>SE</i>s.  If you look at your standardized solution for model 6, you will see many correlations beyond 1 and negative variances, as well as extremely poor fit.  </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">summary</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> std </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br></div><div>Inspecting your data gives you some clues as to why your model is so inappropriate.  <br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">round</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cor</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lavNames</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)]),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> digits </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br></div><div>For example, the indicators of attacking correlation more highly with some indicators of skill than with each other, and that will cause factor correlations to skyrocket to try and compensate.  Also, x3 looks like it should be reverse-coded to go in the same direction as the other indicators.  With a mix of positive and negative factor correlations, having a negative factor loading might cause some trouble on its own, even in a model that at least approximates the data-generating process.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I&#39;ve tried not to fix this two variances. But the program came to an error.</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>That is a problem, because your model should be identified just with the fixed factor loadings.  If a model fits very poorly, the optimizer may never find a &quot;best&quot; solution because no good one exists.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
